what we're doing is trying to take  mutations in different genes and combine  them together to understand how genes  work together to coordinate cell life  and we do this in a simple organism like  yeast that only has six thousand genes  but remarkably we yeast is very similar  to humans so it provides a very good  model to understand so what we learn in  yeast can provide us with some direction  or a reference to understand to help us  understand how genes work together to  modulate essential functions and life in  human cells so what we do is take a  collection of yeast strains each of  which is deleted for a different gene or  mutated for a different gene and we use  a technology we developed in our lab  that allows us to combine pairs of  mutations in the same cell and then we  can simply ask do mutations in these  pairs of genes allow the cell to live or  not and we apply we use robotic  technologies to do this and we were able  to test all 18 million possible mutant  combinations and we don't think that  genes work in isolation so there are  obviously many examples of dizzy  Mendelian diseases where a single  mutation in a single gene results in a  disease so those cases would be highly  predictive we think that many common  diseases are affected by combinations of  mutations rare mutations in different  genes what we've done is tested all  possible double mutant combinations to  build this large Network and we see many  structures in these networks that we  think could provide a reference from  which we can start to learn the rules  and principles that might help us to  understand or simplify the problem in  humans and help us understand how  develop rules how different genes  interact we believe that this will help  us simplify the problem or at least  provide a guide to start to understand  how genes work together to modify  complex disease in humans
 

